In the context of the 'esophageal' database, I have three different queries: `getPatients`, `getDoctors`, and `getAppointments` for the homepage. I want to create a single query that combines these into a single result set. My three simplified queries are as follows:

getPatients = SELECT * FROM patients;
getDoctors = SELECT * FROM doctors;
getAppointments = SELECT * FROM appointments;

I attempted to combine these into a single query as follows:

issue_sql:
DO $$
DECLARE
    v_molecule_id TEXT;
    v_label TEXT;
    v_atom_id TEXT;
    v_element TEXT;
    v_atom_id2 TEXT;
    v_bond_id TEXT;
    v_bond_type TEXT;
BEGIN
    SELECT "m"."molecule_id",
           "m"."label",
           "a"."atom_id",
           "a"."element",
           "c"."atom_id2",
           "b"."bond_id",
           "b"."bond_type"
    FROM "molecule" "m"
    INNER JOIN "atom" "a" ON "m"."molecule_id" = "a"."molecule_id"
    INNER JOIN "connected" "c" ON "a"."atom_id" = "c"."atom_id"
    INNER JOIN "bond" "b" ON "c"."bond_id" = "b"."bond_id"
    ORDER BY "m"."molecule_id",
             "a"."atom_id",
             "c"."atom_id2";
END $$

DO $$
DECLARE v_match_id BIGINT;
v_match_date TEXT;
v_home_team_goals BIGINT;
v_away_team_goals BIGINT;
v_home_team_name TEXT;
v_away_team_name TEXT;
v_league_name TEXT;

BEGIN
SELECT "match"."id",
       "match"."date",
       "match"."home_team_goal",
       "match"."away_team_goal",
       "HomeTeam"."team_long_name",
       "AwayTeam"."team_long_name",
       "league"."name"
FROM "match"
INNER JOIN "team" "HomeTeam" ON "match"."home_team_api_id" = "HomeTeam"."team_api_id"
INNER JOIN "team" "AwayTeam" ON "match"."away_team_api_id" = "AwayTeam"."team_api_id"
INNER JOIN "league" ON "match"."league_id" = "league"."id"
ORDER BY "match"."date";

END $$

